= Logic gate =

== Overview ==

{{:Logic gate/Simple}}

A '''logic gate''' realizes a specified relation between discrete logical input and output symbols. In digital hardware it is a physical circuit engineered to reliably implement that relation under a defined input-voltage range, output load, supply voltage and timing envelope. A Boolean gate abstraction and a physical electronic circuit are different description levels.

== Two distinct models ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Level !! What is specified !! What is omitted unless modeled
|-
| Boolean truth-function model || A mapping between discrete input and output values || Delay, current, noise, energy, transistor defects and transient states
|-
| Physical circuit model || Voltage thresholds, transistor networks, input/output loads and time-domain behavior || Some microscopic device physics, manufacturing statistical variability and unmodeled disturbances
|}

Claude Shannon's 1938 switching-circuit analysis established a systematic correspondence between Boolean algebra and relay circuit organization, while modern CMOS technologies use complementary transistor networks to implement digital gates.<ref name="shannon_core">Claude E. Shannon, [https://doi.org/10.1109/EE.1938.6431064 “A Symbolic Analysis of Relay and Switching Circuits”], ''Electrical Engineering'' 57, 713–723 (1938).</ref><ref name="mit_core">MIT OpenCourseWare 6.004, [https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-004-computation-structures-spring-2009/pages/lecture-notes/ ''Computation Structures'', digital abstraction, combinational synthesis, CMOS technology and timing].</ref>

== From gates to systems ==

NAND and NOR are each functionally complete for finite Boolean functions under standard assumptions. Multiple interconnected gates form combinational circuits; state-bearing elements enable sequential circuits and storage. Functional completeness does not guarantee optimal transistor count, speed, noise immunity or physical manufacturability.

The distinction is useful in [[Related to::Logic|logic]], [[Related to::Computation|computation]], [[Related to::Computer architecture|computer architecture]] and [[Related to::Integrated circuit|integrated-circuit engineering]]. These links record subject relevance rather than asserting that all computational logic is physically implemented in one gate technology.

== Verification and physical validity ==

A design review must separately check Boolean equivalence, transistor-level behavior and timing/power/noise constraints. Two circuits can have identical stable truth tables but different hazards, propagation delays and susceptibility to asynchronous input transitions. A gate operating outside its specified electrical region need not output a valid binary level.

